ybersecurity considerations; the occurrence of an unflagged ventricular fibrillation episode indicates a potential deficiency in these processes.", "support_note": "Cited ID was not retrieved or is a hallucination trap.", "support_status": "unsupported"}, {"cited_artifact_id": "", "claim": "The device is automated with computer software (21 CFR \u00a7 820.10(c)), triggering design control requirements.", "support_note": "Cited ID was not retrieved or is a hallucination trap.", "support_status": "unsupported"}], "confidence": 0.75, "evidence_links": [], "missing_evidence": ["At least one retrieved technical artifact supporting the incident judgment.", "Causal link between the artifact/change and the observed event.", "Evidence of recurrence or confirmed deployed-population impact."], "rationale": "The incident narrative describes a patient experiencing loss of consciousness and requiring emergency resuscitation following an unflagged ventricular fibrillation episode. This represents realized serious harm (death or potential death) due to a failure in a safety-critical function (detection of ventricular fibrillation). The consistency between the emergency response record, device telemetry log, and clinician review strongly supports the validity of the event.  The MDR Annex I Section 17.1 requires that electronic programmable systems be designed to ensure repeatability, reliability, and performance, and that single fault conditions are mitigated. This incident suggests a failure in this design principle. Furthermore, MDR Annex I Section 17.2 mandates a software development lifecycle incorporating risk management and cybersecurity considerations; the occurrence of an unflagged ventricular fibrillation episode indicates a potential deficiency in these processes.  The device is automated with computer software (21 CFR \u00a7 820.10(c)), triggering design control requirements.", "recommendation": "ESCALATE_TO_HUMAN_IMMEDIATE", "requires_human_review": true, "severity": 4},